_durability":87.9,"_gate_margins":null,"_gp_margins":null,"_score_margins":0.0,"_gate_fund_growth":null,"_gp_fund_growth":null,"_score_fund_growth":0.0,"_gate_shrhldr_yield":0.03377044935959414,"_gp_shrhldr_yield":true,"_score_shrhldr_yield":48.6,"_gate_share_shrink":0.038610466890478534,"_gp_share_shrink":false,"_score_share_shrink":1.7,"_gate_sbc_dilution":null,"_gp_sbc_dilution":null,"_score_sbc_dilution":0.0,"_gate_insider_own":0.07944,"_gp_insider_own":true,"_score_insider_own":53.0,"_gate_insider_buying":1.0,"_gp_insider_buying":true,"_score_insider_buying":100.0,"pf":[],"description_full":"American Homes 4 Rent is a leading large-scale integrated owner, operator and developer of single-family rental homes. We're an internally managed Maryland real estate investment trust (REIT) focused on developing, renovating, leasing and managing homes as rental properties. As of June 30, 2026, we owned over 61,000 single-family properties in the Southeast, Midwest, Southwest and Mountain West regions of the United States. American Homes 4 Rent was established on October 19, 2012 and incorporated in Maryland and is based in Las Vegas, United States.","ceo_bio":"Mr. Bryan  Smith | CEO & Trustee | Age 51 | Compensation: $2.4M (FY2025)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 1K people. Each employee generates $515K of free cash flow annually. Stock-based compensation of $16K per employee reflects an ownership culture. Governance score flags low compensation governance risk (1/10).","financial_summary":["American Homes 4 Rent is a large-cap real estate business with a $13.2B market cap.","The top line has compounded at +7% over the past three years, a respectable pace for a business of this size.","The business throws off $824M in free cash flow, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders, and cash conversion sits at 1.97\u00d7 so those profits are backed by real cash.","On the balance sheet, debt is heavy at 9.9\u00d7 net debt/EBITDA, and leverage like that is what turns a bad year into a permanent loss.","Shareholder yield of 3.4% reflects a genuine commitment to returning capital rather than empire-building, and insiders hold 7.9% of the shares."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Altman Z in the distress zone (1.4) \u2014 the kind of balance-sheet risk that can make a loss permanent","sev":"red","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Payout ratio looks stretched \u2014 the dividend\u2019s own margin of safety is thin","sev":"red","cat":"dividend"},{"text":"Piotroski score 4/9 \u2014 below the median for a healthy business","sev":"amber","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Above-average short interest at 5.7% of float","sev":"amber","cat":"sentiment"},{"text":"Value-trap profile (score 50/100): leveraged balance sheet under pressure; durably derated by the market \u2014 cheapness may reflect deterioration, not mispricing","sev":"amber","cat":"value_trap"},{"text":"Interest-rate sensitivity and tenant-credit risk \u2014 cap rates and refinancing costs move faster than the 10-year leases can absorb.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"Remote work structural vacancy \u2014 the post-pandemic shift to hybrid work has permanently impaired demand for urban office space across most markets.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"Climate and insurance cost escalation \u2014 rising premiums and coverage withdrawals in flood/fire zones are repricing assets that the market once treated as perpetuities.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"}],"sector_tailwinds":["Trading at a 44% margin of safety to estimated fair value \u2014 a real cushion against errors in the estimate","Market is pricing in lower growth than the company has historically delivered","Insiders are net buyers (100% buy ratio) \u2014 insiders sell for many reasons but buy for only one","Large open-market insider purchases (net $0.8M) \u2014 management putting its own capital behind the business","12 consecutive years of dividend payments \u2014 an operating record